[{"short_id":"p5w5j6","created_at":"2026-09-05T05:58:49.333-05:00","title":"Trusting-Trust Attack against an Entire Linux Distribution (via the strip utility)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.24888","score":24,"flags":0,"comment_count":1,"description":"\u003cp\u003e\"Ken Thompson's trusting-trust attack, in which a compromised compiler backdoors the programs it builds and reproduces the backdoor in subsequent rebuilds of itself, is widely regarded as a threat specific to compilers. We show that it is not.\"\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA few weeks ago I came across this \u003ca href=\"https://www.teamten.com/lawrence/writings/coding-machines/\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003ehttps://www.teamten.com/lawrence/writings/coding-machines/\u003c/a\u003e : it's clearly fictional (and furthermore is \"pre-AI\" in that it was written in 2009) but is interesting in that it raises the question of whether one or more \u003cem\u003ecomputers\u003c/em\u003e could- without \"intention\" embed code meeting the \"trusting trust\" criteria in GCC.\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"\"Ken Thompson's trusting-trust attack, in which a compromised compiler backdoors the programs it builds and reproduces the backdoor in subsequent rebuilds of itself, is widely regarded as a threat specific to compilers. We show that it is not.\"\r\n\r\nA few weeks ago I came across this https://www.teamten.com/lawrence/writings/coding-machines/ : it's clearly fictional (and furthermore is \"pre-AI\" in that it was written in 2009) but is interesting in that it raises the question of whether one or more *computers* could- without \"intention\" embed code meeting the \"trusting trust\" criteria in GCC.","submitter_user":"MarkMLl","user_is_author":false,"tags":["linux","nix","security"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/p5w5j6","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/p5w5j6/trusting_trust_attack_against_entire"},{"short_id":"7tpc5q","created_at":"2026-09-04T08:11:47.640-05:00","title":"Surviving Code Reviews in the era of AI","url":"","score":121,"flags":2,"comment_count":81,"description":"\u003cp\u003eMy coworkers are all fully AI pilled. As a consequence, every PR that comes my way for review is on average ~6k lines of diff.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIt's well established that even PRs an order of magnitude smaller than this are too big to be effectively reviewed. I'm still clinging to the idea that us humans should understand how the systems we are building work, so I'm hesitant to use AI for reviews aside from more general question answering. Much of what it produces in the way of explanations are difficult to follow and overly verbose anyway.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI'm sure I'm not the only person here who has been dealing with this problem, so I'm curious if anyone here has found any strategies (organizational or personal) which have helped weather this maelstrom.\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"My coworkers are all fully AI pilled. As a consequence, every PR that comes my way for review is on average ~6k lines of diff.\r\n\r\nIt's well established that even PRs an order of magnitude smaller than this are too big to be effectively reviewed. I'm still clinging to the idea that us humans should understand how the systems we are building work, so I'm hesitant to use AI for reviews aside from more general question answering. Much of what it produces in the way of explanations are difficult to follow and overly verbose anyway.\r\n\r\nI'm sure I'm not the only person here who has been dealing with this problem, so I'm curious if anyone here has found any strategies (organizational or personal) which have helped weather this maelstrom.","submitter_user":"mrpossoms","user_is_author":true,"tags":["ask"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/7tpc5q","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/7tpc5q/surviving_code_reviews_era_ai"},{"short_id":"utloro","created_at":"2026-09-04T09:41:18.640-05:00","title":"Is AI ruining my brain?","url":"https://thoughtbot.com/blog/is-ai-ruining-my-brain","score":59,"flags":1,"comment_count":18,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"MatheusRich","user_is_author":false,"tags":["vibecoding"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/utloro","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/utloro/is_ai_ruining_my_brain"},{"short_id":"fxccx9","created_at":"2026-09-05T02:09:14.682-05:00","title":"Is it too much to ask devs to use AI to review their hand-crafted code?","url":"","score":10,"flags":5,"comment_count":67,"description":"\u003cp\u003eYou believe coding by hand is the Way and I both admire and appreciate beautiful, hand-crafted code. But I want to use your library, and when I ask my AI to evaluate it for bugs, memory leaks, security issues, it returns a long list of serious ones.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eEven if you hate AI-generated code, you can use an AI to identify issues and you can fix them yourself. Think of it as an advanced linter or something.\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"You believe coding by hand is the Way and I both admire and appreciate beautiful, hand-crafted code. But I want to use your library, and when I ask my AI to evaluate it for bugs, memory leaks, security issues, it returns a long list of serious ones.\r\n\r\nEven if you hate AI-generated code, you can use an AI to identify issues and you can fix them yourself. Think of it as an advanced linter or something.","submitter_user":"vrypan","user_is_author":true,"tags":["programming","vibecoding"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/fxccx9","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/fxccx9/is_it_too_much_ask_devs_use_ai_review_their"},{"short_id":"o8bp2s","created_at":"2026-09-04T13:33:44.332-05:00","title":"Babashka 1.13.220 gets FFI","url":"https://blog.michielborkent.nl/babashka-ffi.html","score":31,"flags":0,"comment_count":3,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"borkdude","user_is_author":true,"tags":["clojure"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/o8bp2s","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/o8bp2s/babashka_1_1